feat(nightshift): autonomous overnight feature-level dev -- first cut

Add the pd nightshift queue and runner: operator drops vague hopes into a
SQLite-backed intent queue; a runner pops them, plans an autonomous spawn
in a fresh worktree under ~/coding/tmp/nightshift/<id>, and (in this first
cut) prints the plan rather than actually invoking the bypass-mode agent.
The flip from dry-run to really-run is opt-in and requires the wrapper
deny-list to be wired -- documented in docs/proposals/pd-nightshift.md.

Wired end-to-end:
- nightshift_intents SQL table (self-initializing CREATE IF NOT EXISTS)
- lib/nightshift/queue.ts: propose, queue, next (atomic pop), markRunning,
  markComplete, cancel, markReviewed
- lib/nightshift/runner.ts: planRunFor + runNext; builds argv for claude
  --dangerously-skip-permissions and codex --full-auto --sandbox
  workspace-write without ever shelling out the intent text
- cli/commands/nightshift.ts: propose, queue, list, show, run, review,
  cancel
- cli/commands/morning.ts: start-of-day summary table
- pd-fleet.yml: commented nightshift-runner ship for the cron path
- bash/zsh/fish completions for the new commands
- 61 unit tests; tsc clean; bijective-parity 414/414 still pass

Stubbed deliberately:
- The 'really-run' path needs a spawnAdapter that the daemon does not yet
  provide; runner.ts ships the interface, callers print the plan
- The claude pre-tool-use deny-list hook for forbidding push-to-main is
  NOT wired in this PR; codex's --sandbox workspace-write is the only
  enforced bound for the codex backend today
- The pd morning TUI is a plain table; the Bostock-density variant is
  follow-up

Operator-input decisions called out in the proposal:
1. Default backend (recommended: codex, because the sandbox is enforced
   by codex itself, not by a wrapper)
2. Whether the agent opens a PR as it goes, or commits to the branch
   only and waits for pd morning to push (current default: branch-only)


